The remaining npm packages in package.json (vite, typescript, oxlint,
@oxlint/plugins, @rikalabs/oxlint-standards, @shadcn/lint, vnu-jar and the build
plugins) are development tools: they run at build time and are not shipped. The
copy of dmmulroy/anti-slop under tools/oxlint/anti-slop/ is not shipped either; it
carries its own LICENSE, is recorded in README.md, and is inventoried file by
file with a sha256 in tools/oxlint/anti-slop.manifest.json, which
tools/vendor_manifest.py writes from the tree.

Full dependency inventory per release: the `recoverage-python-sbom` and
`recoverage-browser-sbom` artifacts produced by the sbom job in
.github/workflows/ci.yml. The second is this file's list with the exact version
and tarball digest of each package as bun.lock resolved it, since the browser
code above ships compiled and the Python export cannot see it. `make
browser-sbom` prints the same inventory without CI. The same rows are published
beside it as `recoverage-browser-spdx`, an SPDX 2.3 document naming each
package's license identifier and upstream alongside its version and digest, so
a vulnerability scanner reads this half of the tree in the shape it takes
rather than as prose. `make browser-sbom-spdx` prints that document too.

The Python packages a consumer INSTALLS are not listed above, because nothing
is redistributed inside the wheel: they are resolved beside it, each under its
own license, and recoverage's MIT grant does not extend to them. `make
license-inventory` reads those licenses off the installed distributions and
refuses any that is not permissive, so the record is a gate rather than a
claim. The tree is wider than the eight names pyproject.toml declares, because
`rebrew` is a runtime import and brings certifi (MPL-2.0, file-level copyleft),
lief, numpy, tree-sitter and python-flirt with it; the walk follows
`Requires-Dist` so all of them are read rather than assumed.
